          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(P)=-27$\n- **II.** $det(P)=27$\n- **III.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(0-6)+3(-3+4)+4(-3+0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-18+3-12 \\\\  & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=-27\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
