          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $E$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(E)=\\frac{7}{4}$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ - \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ - \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{2}{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{7}|\\begin{aligned}- \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}- \\sqrt{7})+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{21}{4}- \\frac{21}{4})+\\sqrt{7}(- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{7}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4})+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot (- \\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{21}{4}-7 \\\\  & =- \\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=- \\frac{49}{4}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
